title: 'For Cleo: the recursive delete cannot take back a fork that holds its original''s children; a client could mark only the owned ones.'
pid: fd47d81f-c681-4605-b2a2-7e793ec43c5a
body: |2-
  Emacs 'C-u C-c <backspace>' and Neovim '<localleader>dR' mark every
  occurrence below the fork, and a fork's content is the original's
  children, which are foreign, so the save refuses ("Foreign repos can
  only be viewed, not modified"). A recursive delete that skipped
  foreign descendants would take back a fork and the reply under it in
  one gesture. Not built; the clients are yours.
  The AI reader's report did not change. It never counts a child
  marked 'delete' as leaving its parent ('claiming_children_keys' in
  'tools/skg-for-ai/skg_for_ai.py'), so for the case built it now
  agrees with the server, where before it predicted no fork and the
  server asked for one. Two older gaps of the same kind remain, found
  by the reviewer: deleting an owned child that is no overrider from a
  foreign parent forks the parent, and deleting an overrider drawn in
  an owned parent takes the original out of that parent, and the
  report says neither.
